Negative control — clearing a region on a country change reaches the API
(AC-19, parity row L9, review blocker B2).

Removes the two clearance lines from `mapIAddressDataDiff`, so a region cleared
by a country change leaves the diff as `undefined` instead of as an explicit
`null`. The diff still correctly KEEPS the key — `isEqual(previousRegionId,
undefined)` is false and the field really did change — but `undefined` cannot
survive the wire: `parseData`'s `JSON.stringify` drops every `undefined`-valued
key before the body is sent.

The mutant is invisible at every gate that measures the CLIENT side. `parse`
still refetches the new country's regions, still re-resolves `country`, and
still clears `model.address.regionId` — the machine context is correct, the form
control is correct, `country_id` is on the wire, and the PUT succeeds. The only
casualty is the one key the API needed in order to clear the column, so the
server keeps the OLD country's region on an address that now sits in a new
country. Persisted inconsistent data on the exact flow AC-19 claims.

Declared subject — the B2 wire read-back. Open an existing address that HAS a
region, change the country (e.g. US -> UK, where the US region is not in the UK
list), save, and assert on the CAPTURED PUT BODY:
  - the body has a `region_id` key, and its value is `null`
Under the mutant the key is absent from the serialised body entirely.

What does NOT discriminate:
  - asserting `useContext().model.address.regionId` is falsy — the model IS
    cleared under the mutant, to `undefined`. An assertion written as
    `expect(model.address.regionId ?? null).toBe(null)` or `.toBeFalsy()` passes
    on the broken value; that is precisely how the defect reached review with
    `client-address.lookups.int.test.ts` green.
  - asserting the regions list was refetched for the new country
  - asserting `country_id` is on the PUT body — it is, under both
  - assertions taken on a parsed object that never round-tripped through
    `JSON.stringify`: assert on the SERIALISED body, or on key PRESENCE

Choose an address that ALREADY HAS a region. On an address that never had one,
both sides of the diff map the same empty value and `region_id` is correctly
omitted from the payload under fix AND mutant alike — that case cannot
discriminate.

Measured blast radius, suite at the B3 fix (this commit): 1 — the declared
subject alone, zero collateral. Under the mutant the module suite runs 119
passed / 2 failed: this control's own subject
(`client-address.manager-save.int.test.ts`, AC-19/AC-23) plus the standing
AC-20/F6 halt, which fails identically without it.

The seam is `mapIAddressDataDiff` and NOT `mapIAddressData` — that placement is
review blocker B3. `mapIAddressData` is the create-body mapper too, and a create
has nothing to clear: the recorded oracle's region-less POST
(`client-address.e2e-oracle.pre-migration.json`, spec 3, second POST) carries no
`region_id` key at all, under the same `country_id` its sibling POSTs send a
region with. Minting the `null` above the `if (!baseData) return next` early
return therefore put a key on every region-less CREATE that legacy never sent.
This mutant does not touch the create body — it is byte-identical under fix and
mutant — so no AC-24 assertion can go red here; the create's own guard is a
separate read-back on POST key ABSENCE.

